FreeBWS -- the FreeLFS web browser
==================================

WPE WebKit (wpewebkit, libwpe, wpebackend-fdo) in a libflui window: a tab row,
a toolbar with back, forward, reload and an address bar that also searches
(Brave Search by default), star, ad blocker shield, downloads and menu buttons, a
bookmarks bar, and the page as a wl_subsurface of the window. Ad blocker, search
engines, downloads, bookmarks, dark mode and error pages: see "Features" below and
docs/M8-BROWSER.md.

  freebws [URL]     default: freebws://start
  Ctrl-L, F6, Alt-D, a click   address bar, all selected (Enter loads,
                               Escape puts the page's address back, twice
                               returns to the page)
  F5, Ctrl-R        reload     Alt-Left, Alt-Right   back, forward
  An address without a scheme gets https:// when it looks like a host (a dot,
  no spaces; IP addresses and localhost get http://), anything else is a
  search with the chosen engine. The window title is the page title.

Tabs (tabstrip.c draws the row)
  Ctrl-T            new tab (start page, address bar ready)
  Ctrl-W, Ctrl-F4   close the tab (the last one closes the window)
  Ctrl-Shift-T      reopen the tab closed last (the last 8 are kept)
  Ctrl-Tab, Ctrl-PageDown       next tab        Ctrl-Shift-Tab, Ctrl-PageUp   previous
  Alt-1 .. Alt-8    that tab    Alt-9   the last tab (Ctrl-1 .. 9 too)
  Click a tab to select it; the x on the active or hovered tab, or a middle
  click on any tab, closes it; + opens a new tab. A spinner shows a tab that
  is loading, a dimmer title one that was discarded.
  target=_blank and window.open open a tab in front when its page is ready
  (related to the opener, so window.opener and postMessage work: OAuth and
  sign-in popups). Ctrl-click and middle-click on a link open one in the
  background. window.close() closes the popup and selects the page that opened
  it. At 24 tabs the oldest background tab that is not loading is closed (it
  stays in the Ctrl-Shift-T list); when there is none a notice says so.
  All tabs share one WebKitWebContext and the one network process. WebKit sees
  background tabs as hidden (it throttles them). A background tab that was not
  looked at for 30 minutes, or the oldest one when less than a fifth of the memory
  (at most 384 MB) is free, is discarded (the opener of a popup that is still
  open and a tab that is loading are never discarded): its web view is destroyed, its
  back/forward list kept, and selecting it loads the page again (scroll position
  and form input are lost). Tabs that load or play audio are never discarded.

How the page gets on screen
  WebKit's web process draws into a private Wayland display that
  wpebackend-fdo runs inside freebws. Each finished frame arrives in freebws
  as either
    shm     (no GPU, llvmpipe): copied into a wl_shm buffer of the window's
            subsurface;
    dmabuf  (a GPU): the buffer's file descriptors are passed to flcomp with
            zwp_linux_dmabuf_v1, no copy in freebws; flcomp reads them (flcomp
            README, "dmabuf").
  freebws picks dmabuf when flcomp announces tiled modifiers (a real GPU) and has written
  $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/flcomp-dmabuf-planes (its GPU path takes multi-plane buffers, which a Radeon makes:
  an older flcomp would drop the connection at the first frame), else shm. FREEBWS_BUFFERS=shm or
  =dmabuf forces one, so does buffers=shm|dmabuf|auto in ~/.config/freebws/config. Only the active
  tab's frames are shown; the others are dropped (shm: the last frame of a tab is kept to show while
  its page repaints after a switch).
  The dmabuf mode gives wpebackend-fdo an EGLDisplay of its own (GBM on /dev/dri/renderD*, the first
  that works; FREEBWS_RENDER_NODE=/dev/dri/renderD129 picks one): only then does the web process find
  zwp_linux_dmabuf_v1 in the private display and draw with the GPU. (Before release 9 this called
  wpe_fdo_initialize_dmabuf(), which is the "dmabuf pool" mode in which the embedder must allocate
  the buffers itself; the web process's first create_buffer got "no memory" and a real GPU showed a
  blank window.) Safety net: a GPU path that delivers no frame in 12 seconds, or a compositor that
  ends the connection with a protocol error in the first minute, makes the browser write buffers=shm
  and start again with the CPU path.
  Tested on the Radeon 610M (radeonsi, DCC buffers) in a headless flcomp on its render node, and in
  QEMU with virgl (virtio-vga-gl); the bare-metal run of the whole desktop is the user's laptop.

libflui's loop runs inside GLib's main loop as a GSource (fl_app_prepare and
fl_app_dispatch), so WebKit's signals and libflui's input share one thread.

Memory (measured in QEMU at 1 GB and 2 GB, docs/M8-BROWSER.md, "Memory")
  cache model DOCUMENT_VIEWER (no page cache: back reloads), ProcessSwapOn-
  CrossSiteNavigation off (a tab keeps its web process across origins),
  Skia CPU rendering unless a GPU is announced, discarding of background tabs.
  Environment (testing): FREEBWS_CACHE=viewer|document|browser,
  FREEBWS_FEATURES=Name=0|1,... (WebKit features, replaces the default list),
  FREEBWS_MP=limit_mb,conservative,strict,kill,poll_s (memory pressure settings),
  FREEBWS_DISCARD_IDLE=seconds (0: never), FREEBWS_DISCARD_MB=free MB,
  FREEBWS_SHARE=1 (all tabs in one web process: related views).

DSSL
  Pages with DSSL tags (<link rel="stylesheet/dssl">, <link rel="stylesheet"
  type="text/dssl">, <style type="text/dssl">, <script type="text/dssl">) are
  styled natively: libdssl lowers the sheets to CSS in the browser process and
  the page gets <style data-dssl-native> elements and the `dssl-ready` event.
  A sheet that needs the engine (texture, aura, @signal, seen, ..., see
  src/libdssl/dssl.h), a page that scripts `DSSL.` itself, or a style-src CSP
  that blocks inline styles, sends the whole page to the bundled JavaScript
  engine (freebws-dssl://engine/..., never the network). <html data-dssl-mode>
  is "native" or "engine"; stderr gets a "freebws: dssl: ..." line per page.
  FREEBWS_DSSL=engine always uses the engine. Test pages in
  /usr/share/freebws: dssl-native-test.html (native), dssl-test.html (texture:
  engine), tabs-test.html (links for the tab features).

Features
  Ad blocker (on by default)   EasyList, EasyPrivacy and uBlock filters, converted by freebws's own
            converter (abp.c) to WebKit content filters, plus cosmetic filtering, scriptlets and a
            YouTube ad remover. Shield button: blocked count, switch for the site you are on.
            Lists: /usr/share/freebws/lists (baked snapshot, licenses in LICENSES there), newer ones
            weekly in ~/.local/share/freebws/adblock/lists. freebws --adblock-update does it by hand.
            First start: a one-time build of about 10 s (a child process, 400 MB for a moment);
            pages wait for it. FREEBWS_ADB_SKIP=net,css,js,yt leaves a part out (finding what
            breaks a site), FREEBWS_ADB_LOG=1 logs it.
  Search    Brave Search, DuckDuckGo, Google, Startpage, Bing, Ecosia (freebws://settings); address bar and
            start page follow it. Config: ~/.config/freebws/config.
  Downloads Saved to ~/downloads (changeable); toolbar button with a progress ring and a small panel
            (cancel, retry, open, show in folder), freebws://downloads (Ctrl-J). The WPE API cannot
            pause or resume a download.
  Bookmarks Ctrl-D (again removes), bar under the address bar (Ctrl-Shift-B), folders (a dropdown),
            freebws://bookmarks (Ctrl-Shift-O): edit, move, delete, import from the HTML file Firefox
            or Chromium export. Stored in ~/.local/share/freebws/bookmarks.json.
  Dark mode Settings (all pages) and the menu (this site): pages that are light are turned dark with
            a filter, pages that are dark already are left alone. Web pages only.
  Errors    offline, no such server, connection refused, TLS (continue anyway), blocked by the ad
            blocker (open anyway), crashed tab: the "oh no." screen of the crash screen, Enter retries.
  Settings  freebws://settings (menu button).
  Testing   FREEBWS_HOME=DIR puts config, data and cache under DIR, FREEBWS_DATADIR=DIR is
            /usr/share/freebws, FREEBWS_EVAL=js runs a script in each page after 3 s and logs it.

Not there yet: find in page, history, zoom, private window.

Web process sandbox (docs/SECURITY.md)
  wpewebkit is built with ENABLE_BUBBLEWRAP_SANDBOX (since the 2.52.5-2 build; now 2.54.2): WebKit starts every web process
  in bubblewrap: own user, mount, pid and uts namespaces (not ipc: WebKit shares it for WPE), a seccomp filter, private /tmp and runtime
  dir, / not visible (only /usr, /etc, /lib*, /dev/dri and /dev/udmabuf for the GPU, fonts,
  GStreamer data, and the paths added with webkit_web_context_add_path_to_sandbox: here
  /usr/share/freebws and $FREEBWS_SANDBOX_RO); the session bus only through xdg-dbus-proxy. The
  network process is not sandboxed by WebKit: it writes the cache, the cookies and the downloads
  (~/downloads), so downloads need no extra rule. The Wayland link is a socket handed over the IPC
  connection (not a path), so dmabuf and shm buffers need nothing but the render node.
  WebKit aborts the browser if bwrap cannot run, so freebws runs a probe bwrap first:
    sandbox=auto (default)  usable: on. Not usable (no user namespaces, bwrap or xdg-dbus-proxy
                            missing, a container): the web processes run WITHOUT a sandbox, with a
                            WARNING on stderr and the Settings page saying so
    sandbox=on              not usable: freebws exits with the reason
    sandbox=off             off on purpose (warned about)
  Config key "sandbox" or FREEBWS_SANDBOX=auto|on|off. `freebws --sandbox-check` prints the verdict
  (exit 0: usable). A page that crashes the web process at once on a machine where the sandbox is on
  is a sandbox problem to look for in the log first (FREEBWS_SANDBOX=off to compare).
  The probe binds what WebKit binds (/usr/lib, /usr/bin, /lib, /lib64, /bin one by one, never /usr as a
  whole) plus the directory of its own real executable. Up to base-files release 3, /usr on FreeLFS was
  the homes directory and /usr/bin a link to ../sysflfs/bin, so /proc/self/exe was /sysflfs/bin/freebws
  (release 10 did not bind that and reported "bwrap: execvp /sysflfs/bin/freebws: No such file or
  directory"). Now /usr is the real system directory and /sysflfs a link to it.

Popups, user agent, cookies (round 16)
  window.open popups: javascript-can-open-windows-automatically is on, so a popup
  from a script (after an await, seconds after the click) is not dropped. The
  popup's tab is shown at ready-to-show (a fallback after 5 s).
  User agent: useragent=STRING in ~/.config/freebws/config replaces the default,
  which is the Safari form every WebKit browser sends (Mozilla/5.0 (X11; Linux x86_64)
  AppleWebKit/605.1.15 (KHTML, like Gecko) Version/18.4 Safari/605.1.15; SAFARI_UA_VERSION
  in freebws.c). It matches the engine: a Chrome string on WebKit (the default until round
  17) is what bot checks such as Cloudflare Turnstile catch (no window.chrome, no
  userAgentData). No WPE token, which Google refuses. ITP is off and
  challenges.cloudflare.com is never blocked (adblock.c BUILTIN_ALLOW).
  Cookies: cookies=always (default) or cookies=no-third-party (or
  FREEBWS_COOKIES=) sets WebKit's cookie accept policy on the default network
  session. The default keeps all cookies. WEBKIT_COOKIE_POLICY_ACCEPT_NO_THIRD_PARTY
  is not the default because sign-in frames and popups that set cookies for the
  page that opened them broke with it, and it was not checked against a real
  sign-in here. The choice is still open for a test on the laptop.
  count.js (the shield's count) no longer wraps window.fetch. It only watches
  failed sub-resources now.
  The first-start filter gate: a navigation that waits for the filters is held
  until they are ready, and a newer address replaces the held one (the stale one
  is never loaded over the page the user is on).
